# Day 50 — Defend Your Experience: An Adversarial Adjudicator for EB-1A Claims

**Challenge:** ABTalks 60-Day Claude Challenge
**Track:** AI Career Intelligence
**Author:** [Vivek Kumar](https://www.linkedin.com/in/vivekdp/)
**Day:** 50 of 60
**Deliverable:** `defend-your-experience.html` (single self-contained file), Defense Report export, screenshots

---

## 🎯 Objective

Build an adaptive interview defense simulator. The brief is explicit that the goal is **not** to improve a résumé — it is to be able to defend every claim you make about yourself.

I pointed it at the hardest version of that problem I actually have: **an EB-1A petition**, where the reader is a sceptical USCIS adjudicator whose job is to find the weakest link, and where an inflated claim does not just fail — it damages the credibility of every other claim in the filing.

Pressure setting: **consistently adversarial.** Hostile from question one, every claim assumed inflated until evidenced.

---

## 🧱 What I Built

A single self-contained HTML application. You paste your CV, petition draft, expert letter or contribution statement; it extracts every assertion that would have to be defended, then interrogates you on each one and produces a Defense Report.

| Section | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Start** | Drag-and-drop or paste your material; choose your interrogator and criterion emphasis |
| **Claims** | Every assertion extracted, classified by evidence gap, with red-flag language quoted |
| **Defend** | The interrogation. One claim at a time, each question written from your previous answer |
| **Defense Report** | Per-claim defense strength, what breaks and the question that breaks it, remediation plan |
| **Past Sessions** | Saved locally so you can resume or compare runs |

### Grounded in real evidentiary doctrine, not invented interview questions

This is the part that mattered most. The interrogator is built on the actual standards an EB-1A petition is judged against, encoded as data rather than vibes:

- **8 red-flag patterns** — unsupported acclaim, vague importance, future-only significance, employer-only significance, implausible basis of knowledge, legal conclusions in the recommender's mouth, template artifacts, verification-required claims. Each carries *why it matters* and *what cures it*.
- **5 evidence-gap categories** — strong and supported / usable with packaging / weak / missing / risky.
- **10 standard adjudicator challenges** — the questions a sceptical reader actually asks, each labelled with what it tests.
- **The 6-link claim chain** — Claim → why true → how known → what proves it → why unusual → why it matters.
- **4 interrogator personas** — USCIS adjudicator, your own attorney stress-testing before filing, an independent expert deciding whether to sign, and an RFE reviewer looking for grounds to deny.

The single test the whole tool applies: **if every flattering adjective were removed, would the remaining facts still persuade a sceptical reader?** If not, the problem is the evidence, not the wording — and polishing the prose actively hides it.

### Two modes, and the badge tells you which one you are in

- **Live** — claims extracted from your document; every follow-up written from what you just said; a written verdict in the report.
- **Offline** — no Claude connection, so the page runs a deterministic red-flag scan over your text, segments it into candidate claims, prioritises the ten standard challenges by which flags fired, and records your answers into a locally-scored report.

